L19: # Managed Auth
L20: 
L21: Handle website login, reuse session state, and recover eligible connections automatically
L22: 
L23: Copy page Copy page
L24: managed auth handles website login and saves authenticated state to a reusable browser profile for your agents. KERNEL monitors the connection and can attempt automatic reauthentication for eligible flows that can complete without human input. managed auth stores authentication state in cite3†browser profiles . profiles can also persist and reuse browser state without managed auth.
L25: use managed auth when you want KERNEL to orchestrate login and session recovery, and your application can bring the user back when authentication requires their input. use cite4†Fill from Vault when your application or agent needs to control navigation, credential filling, submission, and recovery in its current browser session.
L26: with automatic recovery enabled, KERNEL can attempt to sign in again when a health check confirms that a session has expired. recovery isn’t guaranteed. if the site requires an email or sms code, approval, or another user action, your application must bring the user back to complete a new login. see cite5†connection lifecycle for eligibility and recovery details.
L27: ##
L28: 
L29: cite6†​ L30: 
L31: How it works
L32: 
L33: 1
L34: 
L35: Create a Connection
L36: 
L37: A Managed Auth Connection attaches a domain’s authentication state to a browser cite3†profile so future browsers can reuse it. A single profile can have multiple auth connections, one per domain.
L38: 
L39: TypeScript
L40: 
L41: Python
L42: Go
L43: 
L44:     `const auth = await kernel.auth.connections.create({
L45:       domain: 'netflix.com',
L46:       profile_name: 'netflix-user-123',
L47:     });
L48:     `
L49: 
L50:     `auth = await kernel.auth.connections.create(
L51:         domain="netflix.com",
L52:         profile_name="netflix-user-123",
L53:     )
L54:     `
L55: 
L56:     `auth, err := client.Auth.Connections.New(ctx, kernel.AuthConnectionNewParams{
L57:     	ManagedAuthCreateRequest: kernel.ManagedAuthCreateRequestParam{
L58:     		Domain:      "netflix.com",
L59:     		ProfileName: "netflix-user-123",
L60:     	},
L61:     })
L62:     if err != nil {
L63:     	panic(err)
L64:     }
L65:     _ = auth
L66:     `
L67: 2
L68: 
L69: Start a Login Session
L70: 
L71: A Managed Auth Session is the corresponding login flow for the specified connection. Users provide credentials via a KERNEL-hosted page or your own UI.link a cite7†credential so KERNEL can attempt reauthentication when the connection is eligible. stored credentials alone don’t make every flow eligible.
L72: 
