L188: these steps establish the initial connection. your integration must also handle `NEEDS_AUTH`: start a new interactive login and bring the user back when a code, choice, or approval is required. wait for successful authentication before resuming work that requires the account. periodic health checks and eligible automatic reauthentication attempts don’t replace this recovery path. see cite5†connection lifecycle for runtime behavior and configuration options.
L189: ##
L190: 
L191: cite8†​ L192: 
L193: Choose your integration
L194: 
L195: ## Hosted UI
L196: 
L197: Start here - Simplest integration Redirect users to KERNEL’s hosted page. Add features incrementally: save credentials for eligible automatic reauthentication, set custom login URLs, and configure SSO.
L198: 
L199: ## React Component
L200: 
L201: Embed in your app - Drop-in component Mount `<KernelManagedAuth />` on a route in your own app. Same flow as Hosted UI, rendered on your origin and trivial to restyle to match your brand.
L202: ## Programmatic
L203: 
L204: Custom Managed Auth UI - Custom UI or headless Build your own credential collection. Handle login fields, SSO buttons, MFA selection, and external actions (push notifications, security keys).
L205: ##
L206: 
L207: cite9†​ L208: 
L209: Connection options
L210: 
L211: Every connection uses the same options, whichever integration you choose. See cite10†configuration for examples of each.
L212: Option  | What it does
L213: --- | ---
L214: cite7†Credentials | Save login values during the first login, or store them ahead of time, so KERNEL can attempt eligible reauthentication.
L215: cite11†Automatic reauthentication | Run health checks and attempt to reauthenticate eligible connections when a session expires, controlled by `health_checks` and `auto_reauth`.
L216: cite12†Custom login URL | Start the login from a specific page instead of the domain’s default.
L217: cite13†Browser region | Run the login and health checks in the region your browsers use.
L218: cite14†SSO and OAuth | Support “Sign in with Google, GitHub, or Microsoft” flows. Common identity providers are allowed by default.
L219: cite15†Custom proxy | Send login traffic through a proxy you choose.
L220: cite16†Session recording | Record login attempts as replays for debugging.
L221: cite17†Post-login URL | Read the page the login landed on, so your automation starts from the right place.
L222: ##
L223: 
L224: cite18†​ L225: 
L226: Why Managed Auth?
L227: 
L228: Managed Auth runs login flows by navigating login pages, filling credentials, following SSO redirects, and guiding users through additional authentication steps. It saves the resulting session state to a reusable profile. The most valuable workflows live behind logins. Managed Auth provides:
L229:   * Broad site coverage - Login pages are discovered and handled across common website login flows
L230:   * SSO/OAuth support - KERNEL follows common SSO redirects. Common provider domains are allowed by default; add custom provider domains to `allowed_domains`
L231:   * 2FA/OTP handling - KERNEL can generate totp codes when the credential includes a totp secret. email and sms codes, approvals, and other user-required steps need an interactive login
L232:   * Post-login URL - Get the URL where login landed (`post_login_url`) so you can start automations from the right page
L233:   * Session monitoring - cite5†Periodic health checks and eligible automatic reauthentication attempts
L234:   * Secure by default - Credentials are encrypted at rest and never exposed in API responses or passed to LLMs
L235: ##
L236: 
L237: cite19†​ L238: 
L239: Security
L240: 
L241: Feature  | Description
L242: --- | ---
L243: Encrypted credentials  | Values encrypted with per-organization keys
L244: No credential exposure  | Never returned in API responses or passed to LLMs
L245: Encrypted profiles  | Browser session state encrypted end-to-end
L246: Isolated execution  | Each login runs in an isolated browser environment
L247: ##
L248: 
L249: cite20†​ L250: 
L251: When to use Fill from Vault
L252: 
L253: Use cite4†Fill from Vault when an end user owns the credentials, remains present during the task, and might need to respond to an authentication prompt mid-workflow. Your application or agent controls navigation, chooses the fields to fill, submits the form, and handles the site’s response. KERNEL collects and stores sensitive values, then fills them without returning them through the api.
L254: 
